E. Numerical methods
Parameter inference is done using linear regression on US Census data. We first interpolate the US Census data using a 3rd-order spline to estimate populations between decennial census years. We then estimate time derivatives and spatial gradients using finite differences. A feature matrix A is constructed using each term in (51) as a column, and a target vector b is created using the time derivatives. The coefficients in (51) are collected in a vector x, and we invert the equation Ax = b. For the simulations shown in Fig. 4, we solve (51) over county boundaries using a finite-volume method. We construct a triangular mesh over the county geometry using GMSH, and use the Python package FiPy [127] to solve (51). More details can be found in the SI. For the simulations shown in Fig. 5, we simulate (51) in 1 spatial dimension using a finite difference method, with a 4th order discretization in space and 1st order discretization in time. Unless otherwise stated, we set T = 0.1 and Γ = 1, and use a time step of ∆t = 0.1 and ∆x = 0.625.
